│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_alternating_entanglement                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ This approach replaces the sequential fixed-layer architecture with an adaptive           │
│                          │ entanglement strategy using alternating basis rotations and varied two-qubit gate types.  │
│                          │ Instead of global shared phase layers, each qubit gets individualized basis rotations in  │
│                          │ different bases (RX, RY, RZ in sequence). The entanglement layer uses a mix of CRX, CRY,  │
│                          │ CRZ with unique parameters (no over-sharing) to maximize expressivity per parameter.      │
│                          │ Three-qubit gates are placed strategically on diverse qubit triples. This design:         │
│                          │ - Reduces generalization gap through less parameter sharing and more diverse connectivity │
│                          │ - Improves test accuracy via better expressivity with fewer parameters                    │
│                          │ - Maintains computational efficiency with reduced circuit depth                           │
│                          │ - Enables cleaner gradient flow without redundant shared parameters                       │
│                          │ The algorithm trades global parameter sharing for local expressivity and diversity, which │
│                          │ typically reduces overfitting in quantum classifiers.                                     │
